# STACKIT Kubernetes Platform: **${platform_identifier}**

%{ if playground_mode ~}
> **Playground mode.** The platform identifier carries a random suffix so this deployment does not
> occupy a name for good, and the STACKIT project and its tenant are left destroyable. Do not publish
> this platform or the building block definitions it registered to other workspaces. Redeploy with
> `playground_mode` set to false for a platform that is actually used.

%{ endif ~}
## Details

| Property | Value |
|----------|-------|
| **STACKIT Project** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](https://portal.stackit.cloud/projects/${stackit_project_id}) (`${stackit_project_id}`) |
| **Secrets Manager** | [Open in STACKIT Portal](https://portal.stackit.cloud/secrets-manager/instances/${secrets_manager_instance_id}/overview?project=${stackit_project_id}) |
| **SKE Cluster** | `${cluster_name}` — @buildingblock[${cluster_bb_uuid}] |
| **Cluster Admin Service Account** | @buildingblock[${service_account_bb_uuid}] |
| **Ingress & Certificates** | `${ingress_load_balancer_ip}`%{ if ingress_bb_uuid != "" } — @buildingblock[${ingress_bb_uuid}]%{ endif } |
| **meshPlatform Credentials** | @buildingblock[${kubernetes_platform_bb_uuid}] |
| **STACKIT Git** | [${forgejo_instance_url}](${forgejo_instance_url}) — @buildingblock[${git_bb_uuid}] |
| **Forgejo Organization** | [`${forgejo_organization}`](${forgejo_instance_url}/${forgejo_organization}) |
| **Container Registry** | [${registry_host}/${registry_name}](${registry_url}) — @buildingblock[${container_registry_bb_uuid}] |
| **DNS Zone** | `${dns_zone_name}`%{ if dns_bb_uuid != "" } — @buildingblock[${dns_bb_uuid}]%{ endif } |
| **AI Model Serving** | `${ai_model}` — @buildingblock[${ai_bb_uuid}] |
| **meshStack Platform** | `${platform_identifier}` |

%{ if !phase2_completed ~}
> [!WARNING]
> ## One step left: create the Harbor robot account

The registry `${registry_name}` exists and every member of this project can open it, but it has no
robot account yet. Until it has one, none of the three definitions application teams order is
registered: their pipelines push an image and their workloads pull it, so without credentials they
would be orderable and what they produce could not start.

Only the portal can create the first robot. Do this once:

1. Open the registry's [Robot Accounts](${registry_robot_url}) and sign in with your STACKIT
   account.
2. Choose **New Robot Account**, name it `bootstrap`, and give it **robot account management**
   permissions. This one robot exists only to create the others; the registry mints a push robot and
   a pull robot from it, and those are what pipelines and workloads use.
3. Link it to this platform's STACKIT service account, entering exactly:

   | Field | Value |
   |---|---|
   | Service account email | `${platform_service_account_email}` |
   | Service account ID | `${platform_service_account_id}` |

4. Set **Phase 2 Completed** to true on the **STACKIT Kubernetes Platform** building block. Its
   next run has the @buildingblock[${container_registry_bb_uuid}] building block find the link,
   using the service account's own token, and mint a push and a pull robot from the bootstrap one.
   Then it registers the three definitions. You do not need the bootstrap robot's secret: Harbor
   ignores it entirely.

The run fails while no robot is linked. Nothing already provisioned is touched.
%{ else ~}
> [!TIP]
> ## Image push and pull are wired up

The Harbor robot for `${registry_name}` is linked, so the three definitions application teams order
are registered, and their pipelines push and their workloads pull private images.
%{ endif ~}

## What application teams get

The **${platform_identifier}** platform is published with one landing zone per stage: ${stage_names}.
Application teams request a Kubernetes namespace on SKE by ordering a tenant in one of them from the
self-service catalog.

| Definition | What a team gets |
|---|---|
| **SKE Starter Kit** | a project, an application repository and a running namespace per stage, from one order |
| **STACKIT Git Repository** | a repository in the `${forgejo_organization}` organization, on its own |

The starter kit is the one to point teams at. It orders the repository for them and wires it to their
namespaces through the **SKE Forgejo Connector**, a third definition that carries the CI/CD plumbing.
Nobody orders the connector directly.

%{ if phase2_completed ~}
Application hostnames are built under `${dns_zone_name}`.
%{ else ~}
Neither is registered yet.
%{ endif ~}
